# --- Secrets -------------------------------------------------------------------------
# `.env` on its own matched only that exact name. A `.env.local` or a `.env.production`
# would have been committed by the next `git add -A`, which is how this goes wrong: not
# by adding a secret on purpose, but by adding a file nobody thought was one.
.env
.env.*
!.env.example

# Keystores and their passwords, wherever they are put. `.wrasse/` covers today's layout
# and these cover a copy made somewhere else at three in the morning.
*.key
*.pem
*.p12
*keystore*
*.password
secrets/

.venv/
__pycache__/
*.py[cod]
.pytest_cache/
.coverage
htmlcov/
dist/
build/
*.egg-info/

# --- Local runtime state and generated demo output -----------------------------------
# Both memories, the transaction ledger and the operational quote. Ignored because they are
# this machine's runtime state, NOT because they are disposable.
#
# An earlier version of this comment called them rebuildable from chain receipts. They are
# not. Supplied receipts can be reverified and replayed, and that is the whole of the claim:
# the learned dimensions carry the severity and confidence every term is scored against and
# do not come back from a log, automatic full-history discovery is explicitly out of scope,
# and the transaction ledger is not reconstructed at all. Deleting `.wrasse/` loses the demo.
#
# The frozen sample a reader is meant to check lives at docs/examples/policy.schema3.json and
# is tracked. `policy.json` here is operational output that changes on every run.
.wrasse/
*.db
*.db-journal
*.db-wal
policy.json

# --- Foundry --------------------------------------------------------------------------
contracts/cache/
contracts/out/
contracts/broadcast/
contracts/lib/

# The unpacked application document. `web/bundle.py unpack` regenerates it from `index.html`
# in a second, and committing it would create a second source of truth that goes stale without
# anyone noticing. The bundle is the artefact; this is a working copy.
web/src/
.service.pid
